trace.content:
[THOUGHT] The lab and imaging findings are highly informative: RF is negative, CRP is normal (arguing against active systemic inflammation), ESR is only slightly elevated (non-specific), and X-rays show classic osteoarthritic changes — joint space narrowing, osteophytes, subchondral sclerosis (both wrists and knees), plus subchondral cysts in the knees. These findings strongly favor chronic degenerative joint disease over inflammatory arthritis. However, the 50-minute morning stiffness remains atypical for primary OA — which usually causes <30 minutes of stiffness. This discrepancy warrants careful physical examination to assess for: (1) bony enlargement (Heberden’s/Bouchard’s nodes, knee osteophytes), (2) crepitus (confirming cartilage loss), (3) absence of soft-tissue swelling or warmth (to reinforce lack of synovitis), and (4) range-of-motion limitations consistent with structural OA. Physical exam is now essential to corroborate imaging and clarify whether the stiffness reflects mechanical gelling vs. low-grade inflammation.

[TEST] Please perform and report findings of a targeted physical examination of both wrists and knees, including:  
- Presence/absence of bony enlargements (e.g., Heberden’s/Bouchard’s nodes in wrists, bony spurs around knees);  
- Palpable crepitus during active or passive motion;  
- Active and passive range of motion (noting any limitation);  
- Tenderness to palpation over joint lines, bony margins, or periarticular structures;  
- Presence/absence of soft-tissue swelling, warmth, or fluid fluctuation.

